 The informed prior defaults to the mean of the three conditioning wavelet tensors (`--prior-sigma 0`). Positive `--prior-sigma` adds Gaussian noise to the prior endpoint once, in image space before DWT. The same prior configuration is stored in the checkpoint and used for sampling. Positive noise must be present during training if stochastic synthesis is desired.
 
+The legacy noiseless default also admits a synthesis failure: the interior state reveals the training velocity, while its learned continuation at the prior endpoint can return nearly zero correction. Positive endpoint noise removes this exact shortcut; its scale still requires validation. `--time-feature-scale 1` enables unit-scale continuous time features for controlled experiments, while the legacy default and old checkpoints retain 1000. Changing either the prior law or time-feature scale requires a new experiment; full checkpoint resume rejects these changes.
+
 WiMF keeps model weights, Adam/EMA state, inputs, wavelets, times and loss arithmetic in float32. `--tf32 true` permits TensorFloat-32 multiplication for CUDA matrix operations and cuDNN convolutions; it accelerates FP32 tensors without enabling FP16/BF16 autocast. Multiplication rounding differs from strict IEEE FP32; `--tf32 false` selects the strict path. See [PyTorch's precision documentation](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.11/notes/cuda.html#tensorfloat-32-tf32-on-ampere-and-later-devices).
 
 `--efficient-loss true` computes the stopped JVP correction without saving reverse-mode activations, evaluates it only for `r<t` examples, and then recomputes the ordinary full-batch prediction with gradients. At `r=t` the correction is exactly zero. With the deterministic WFM backbone this preserves iMF's loss and parameter gradients at the same precision, up to floating-point roundoff; it changes execution order, not the prior, time mixture, or sampling equation. `--efficient-loss false` retains the reference path and its `u/v_pred/dudt` diagnostics for equation checks. Older checkpoints missing either execution flag inherit `false` unless explicitly overridden.
